1
00:00:00,542 --> 00:00:02,640
Is your cat secretly a genius?

2
00:00:03,032 --> 00:00:05,608
Some people say cats are too independent

3
00:00:05,504 --> 00:00:06,293
to be smart.

4
00:00:06,662 --> 00:00:09,361
But scientists have been testing cat brains,

5
00:00:09,599 --> 00:00:11,237
and the results are surprising.

6
00:00:12,244 --> 00:00:15,950
Cats remember, count, read our faces, follow

7
00:00:15,864 --> 00:00:17,955
where we point, and even learn each

8
00:00:17,855 --> 00:00:18,783
other's names.

9
00:00:19,846 --> 00:00:21,699
So today, let's find out how smart

10
00:00:21,605 --> 00:00:23,729
your cat really is, and what's going

11
00:00:23,665 --> 00:00:25,673
on inside that little furry head.

12
00:00:26,702 --> 00:00:28,667
Chapter one: the cat brain.

13
00:00:29,004 --> 00:00:31,609
A cat's brain weighs only about thirty

14
00:00:31,568 --> 00:00:34,256
grams, less than one percent of its

15
00:00:34,163 --> 00:00:34,987
body weight.

16
00:00:35,992 --> 00:00:37,390
But size isn't everything.

17
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:41,050
What matters most is the cortex, the

18
00:00:41,102 --> 00:00:43,283
thinking part of the brain, and how

19
00:00:43,204 --> 00:00:44,642
many neurons it has.

20
00:00:45,788 --> 00:00:47,736
Cats have about two hundred and fifty

21
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:50,014
million neurons in their cortex.

22
00:00:50,422 --> 00:00:53,050
Dogs have about twice as many, and

23
00:00:52,969 --> 00:00:54,969
humans have around sixteen billion.

24
00:00:55,969 --> 00:00:58,135
That might sound small, but cat brains

25
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:00,581
are wired beautifully for what cats do

26
00:01:00,538 --> 00:01:04,510
best: hunting, problem solving and paying close

27
00:01:04,429 --> 00:01:04,999
attention.

28
00:01:06,005 --> 00:01:07,727
In fact, the parts of the cat

29
00:01:07,665 --> 00:01:10,251
brain that handle vision and hearing are

30
00:01:10,186 --> 00:01:11,582
extremely developed.

31
00:01:11,933 --> 00:01:14,821
Your cat's brain is a precision hunting

32
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:15,289
machine.

33
00:01:16,294 --> 00:01:17,546
And cats sleep a lot.

34
00:01:17,951 --> 00:01:21,021
Scientists think sleep helps brains store what

35
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,152
they learned during the day, so all

36
00:01:23,067 --> 00:01:24,597
those naps may be part of the

37
00:01:24,525 --> 00:01:24,987
plan.

38
00:01:26,035 --> 00:01:27,871
Chapter two: memory.

39
00:01:28,249 --> 00:01:30,136
Cats have good long-term memory.

40
00:01:30,478 --> 00:01:33,661
They remember people, places and routines for

41
00:01:33,622 --> 00:01:34,328
years.

42
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:38,827
Ever noticed how your cat knows exactly

43
00:01:38,771 --> 00:01:40,318
which cupboard has the treats?

44
00:01:40,673 --> 00:01:42,678
Or hides the moment it sees the

45
00:01:42,596 --> 00:01:43,555
cat carrier?

46
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:45,205
That's memory at work.

47
00:01:46,224 --> 00:01:49,409
Their short-term memory, or working memory, is

48
00:01:49,353 --> 00:01:50,038
shorter.

49
00:01:50,423 --> 00:01:53,139
In some experiments, cats remembered where an

50
00:01:53,082 --> 00:01:55,226
object was hidden for up to about

51
00:01:55,107 --> 00:01:55,698
a minute.

52
00:01:56,743 --> 00:01:58,861
That's why distracting a cat works so

53
00:01:58,819 --> 00:01:59,281
well.

54
00:01:59,649 --> 00:02:02,014
Move the toy, and a few moments

55
00:02:01,948 --> 00:02:04,409
later, the old one may be forgotten.

56
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:08,523
Cats also seem to have episodic-like memory.

57
00:02:08,886 --> 00:02:11,613
In a 2020 study, cats remembered which

58
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:14,369
bowls they had already eaten from, and

59
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:15,808
which ones still had food.

60
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:19,633
So your cat doesn't just remember facts.

61
00:02:20,027 --> 00:02:23,040
It remembers what happened, where, and maybe

62
00:02:22,969 --> 00:02:23,703
even when.

63
00:02:24,718 --> 00:02:27,146
Cats also remember bad experiences.

64
00:02:27,498 --> 00:02:28,627
That's why a cat that had a

65
00:02:28,557 --> 00:02:31,073
scary vet visit may hide the next

66
00:02:30,989 --> 00:02:32,649
time it hears the car keys.

67
00:02:33,737 --> 00:02:36,440
Chapter three: the cat physicist.

68
00:02:36,805 --> 00:02:38,153
If you hide a toy behind a

69
00:02:38,085 --> 00:02:41,082
pillow, your cat knows the toy still

70
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:41,787
exists.

71
00:02:42,158 --> 00:02:44,168
This is called object permanence.

72
00:02:45,231 --> 00:02:48,243
Human babies develop it around eight months

73
00:02:48,195 --> 00:02:48,985
to a year.

74
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,458
Cats master it as kittens, because hunters

75
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:55,043
must track prey that disappears behind a

76
00:02:54,992 --> 00:02:55,611
bush.

77
00:02:56,670 --> 00:02:58,519
Cats also seem to understand a bit

78
00:02:58,408 --> 00:02:59,258
of physics.

79
00:02:59,624 --> 00:03:01,996
In one Japanese study, cats heard a

80
00:03:01,935 --> 00:03:04,977
rattling sound in a container, then watched

81
00:03:04,873 --> 00:03:06,298
it being tipped over.

82
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:09,695
When nothing fell out, even though they

83
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:12,971
had heard something inside, the cats stared

84
00:03:12,904 --> 00:03:13,563
longer.

85
00:03:13,932 --> 00:03:15,953
They expected an object to drop.

86
00:03:16,995 --> 00:03:19,509
In other words, your cat uses sound

87
00:03:19,405 --> 00:03:21,717
to predict what's hidden inside things.

88
00:03:22,076 --> 00:03:24,152
That's very useful when your dinner is

89
00:03:24,054 --> 00:03:25,422
a mouse in the grass.

90
00:03:26,468 --> 00:03:28,948
And yes, this also explains why your

91
00:03:28,894 --> 00:03:31,886
cat knows exactly which bag has the

92
00:03:31,802 --> 00:03:33,835
treats, just from the sound.

93
00:03:34,877 --> 00:03:37,045
Many owners have also seen their cats

94
00:03:36,989 --> 00:03:39,088
learn to open doors by jumping on

95
00:03:38,962 --> 00:03:39,631
the handle.

96
00:03:40,013 --> 00:03:41,947
Cats learn a lot just by watching.

97
00:03:42,972 --> 00:03:44,706
Some cats even learn to turn on

98
00:03:44,689 --> 00:03:48,251
taps, flush toilets, or open drawers to

99
00:03:48,165 --> 00:03:49,014
get what they want.

100
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:51,902
Nobody teaches them step by step.

101
00:03:52,291 --> 00:03:55,404
They watch, they experiment, and they remember

102
00:03:55,295 --> 00:03:55,974
what worked.

103
00:03:56,335 --> 00:03:59,243
That is trial and error learning, the

104
00:03:59,163 --> 00:04:01,706
same kind of problem solving scientists study

105
00:04:01,715 --> 00:04:04,353
in clever animals like crows and monkeys.

106
00:04:05,371 --> 00:04:07,621
Chapter four: can cats count?

107
00:04:07,976 --> 00:04:08,779
Not like we do.

108
00:04:09,189 --> 00:04:11,086
But they can tell small amounts apart.

109
00:04:12,085 --> 00:04:14,519
In experiments, cats learned to choose a

110
00:04:14,462 --> 00:04:17,158
group of two dots or three dots,

111
00:04:17,575 --> 00:04:20,765
and kept choosing correctly, even when the

112
00:04:20,687 --> 00:04:22,287
dots changed size.

113
00:04:23,365 --> 00:04:25,816
This ability probably helps in the wild,

114
00:04:26,053 --> 00:04:28,293
to choose the bigger group of prey,

115
00:04:28,779 --> 00:04:30,418
or to avoid the bigger group of

116
00:04:30,344 --> 00:04:31,037
rivals.

117
00:04:32,069 --> 00:04:33,674
So your cat may not notice if

118
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:35,399
you give it two scoops of food

119
00:04:35,503 --> 00:04:36,459
instead of three.

120
00:04:36,831 --> 00:04:38,274
Actually, it probably does.

121
00:04:39,343 --> 00:04:42,104
Scientists still don't know exactly how far

122
00:04:42,056 --> 00:04:42,886
this goes.

123
00:04:43,272 --> 00:04:46,674
Testing cats is hard, and most studies

124
00:04:46,613 --> 00:04:48,589
only use a small number of cats.

125
00:04:49,671 --> 00:04:51,951
Chapter five: reading people.

126
00:04:52,339 --> 00:04:55,545
Cats are much better at understanding humans

127
00:04:55,618 --> 00:04:57,079
than we give them credit for.

128
00:04:58,104 --> 00:05:00,800
In a 2005 study, cats were able

129
00:05:00,698 --> 00:05:03,294
to follow human pointing to find hidden

130
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:05,898
food, almost as well as dogs.

131
00:05:06,977 --> 00:05:09,080
Cats also use social referencing.

132
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:11,968
When they see something new and scary,

133
00:05:12,417 --> 00:05:14,299
many cats look at their owner first,

134
00:05:14,465 --> 00:05:15,713
to see how they react.

135
00:05:16,762 --> 00:05:19,784
In one study, when owners sounded calm

136
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:22,876
and positive about a strange object, cats

137
00:05:22,837 --> 00:05:24,232
tended to relax.

138
00:05:24,621 --> 00:05:27,198
When owners sounded scared, cats were more

139
00:05:27,127 --> 00:05:27,970
cautious.

140
00:05:29,012 --> 00:05:31,619
Cats can also tell when we're smiling

141
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:34,727
or frowning, and some research suggests they're

142
00:05:34,651 --> 00:05:36,864
a bit more affectionate when their owner

143
00:05:36,762 --> 00:05:37,564
looks happy.

144
00:05:38,596 --> 00:05:42,145
And when offered food, toys, scents and

145
00:05:42,039 --> 00:05:45,095
human attention, many cats in one study

146
00:05:45,007 --> 00:05:46,695
picked human attention first.

147
00:05:47,739 --> 00:05:49,437
So your cat is paying attention to

148
00:05:49,378 --> 00:05:51,211
you, much more than it lets you

149
00:05:51,165 --> 00:05:51,629
see.

150
00:05:52,672 --> 00:05:54,913
They also learn your schedule: when you

151
00:05:54,818 --> 00:05:57,517
wake up, when you come home, and

152
00:05:57,398 --> 00:05:58,725
when you usually feed them.

153
00:05:59,738 --> 00:06:02,121
Chapter six: words and names.

154
00:06:02,527 --> 00:06:05,389
Cats can recognize their own name, even

155
00:06:05,281 --> 00:06:06,698
when a stranger says it.

156
00:06:07,739 --> 00:06:10,145
A 2022 study went further.

157
00:06:10,515 --> 00:06:13,171
Cats that lived with other cats seemed

158
00:06:13,072 --> 00:06:14,858
to know the names of their feline

159
00:06:14,796 --> 00:06:15,721
housemates.

160
00:06:16,755 --> 00:06:19,272
When they heard one cat's name, and

161
00:06:19,173 --> 00:06:20,960
then saw a photo of a different

162
00:06:20,899 --> 00:06:23,258
cat, they looked at the screen longer,

163
00:06:23,665 --> 00:06:25,044
as if they were surprised.

164
00:06:26,086 --> 00:06:28,695
Cats also react more when we talk

165
00:06:28,611 --> 00:06:31,213
to them in that special high-pitched voice

166
00:06:31,274 --> 00:06:32,491
we use for pets.

167
00:06:33,572 --> 00:06:34,850
But only when it's their owner.

168
00:06:35,206 --> 00:06:36,974
When a stranger used the same cat

169
00:06:36,940 --> 00:06:39,448
voice, the cats didn't care much.

170
00:06:40,523 --> 00:06:43,082
So yes, that silly voice you use

171
00:06:43,007 --> 00:06:45,034
with your cat actually means something to

172
00:06:44,988 --> 00:06:45,242
it.

173
00:06:46,307 --> 00:06:48,838
Many cats also learn words linked to

174
00:06:48,742 --> 00:06:52,182
their routine, like treat, dinner or vet.

175
00:06:52,582 --> 00:06:54,654
Some owners even have to spell those

176
00:06:54,574 --> 00:06:55,544
words out loud.

177
00:06:56,618 --> 00:06:58,797
Chapter seven: the mental map.

178
00:06:59,211 --> 00:07:00,924
Cats keep track of where we are,

179
00:07:01,425 --> 00:07:03,019
even when they can't see us.

180
00:07:04,081 --> 00:07:06,680
In a 2021 study, cats heard their

181
00:07:06,597 --> 00:07:09,511
owner's voice from one speaker, and then

182
00:07:09,417 --> 00:07:11,858
suddenly from another speaker far away.

183
00:07:12,903 --> 00:07:15,410
The cats looked surprised, as if their

184
00:07:15,311 --> 00:07:16,617
owner had teleported.

185
00:07:16,973 --> 00:07:18,914
That suggests they were picturing where their

186
00:07:18,815 --> 00:07:19,671
owner was.

187
00:07:20,738 --> 00:07:22,344
That's a skill once thought to be

188
00:07:22,277 --> 00:07:23,502
mostly human.

189
00:07:23,869 --> 00:07:26,544
Your cat is quietly keeping a map

190
00:07:26,482 --> 00:07:28,338
of your home, and of you.

191
00:07:29,337 --> 00:07:32,000
This mental map also helps explain how

192
00:07:31,981 --> 00:07:35,389
cats move around the house so confidently,

193
00:07:35,816 --> 00:07:36,737
even in the dark.

194
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:40,416
Your cat's mental map also includes time.

195
00:07:40,774 --> 00:07:43,754
Many cats know exactly which sunny spot

196
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:45,705
gets warm in the morning and which

197
00:07:45,648 --> 00:07:47,872
window gets the afternoon light.

198
00:07:48,213 --> 00:07:50,617
They plan their naps around it, moving

199
00:07:50,515 --> 00:07:52,964
from spot to spot as the sun

200
00:07:52,946 --> 00:07:54,180
crosses the house.

201
00:07:54,557 --> 00:07:56,764
It looks lazy, but it is actually

202
00:07:56,668 --> 00:07:58,118
a pretty smart schedule.

203
00:07:59,173 --> 00:08:01,386
Chapter eight: so why do cats seem

204
00:08:01,302 --> 00:08:02,883
less smart than dogs?

205
00:08:03,283 --> 00:08:04,631
A big part of the answer is

206
00:08:04,566 --> 00:08:07,297
that cats are terrible test takers.

207
00:08:08,333 --> 00:08:11,005
Researchers often joke that the hardest part

208
00:08:10,898 --> 00:08:13,225
of studying cats is getting them to

209
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:13,819
show up.

210
00:08:14,184 --> 00:08:17,216
Many cats simply refuse to participate.

211
00:08:18,253 --> 00:08:20,742
Dogs were bred for thousands of years

212
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:23,716
to work with humans and follow instructions.

213
00:08:24,086 --> 00:08:26,052
Cats were mostly left to hunt on

214
00:08:25,964 --> 00:08:26,521
their own.

215
00:08:27,586 --> 00:08:29,599
So a cat ignoring you doesn't mean

216
00:08:29,534 --> 00:08:30,674
it didn't understand.

217
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:34,014
It often means it understood perfectly, and

218
00:08:33,945 --> 00:08:36,104
decided it had better things to do.

219
00:08:37,219 --> 00:08:40,223
Cat intelligence is independent intelligence.

220
00:08:40,543 --> 00:08:43,474
It's built for solving problems alone, not

221
00:08:43,396 --> 00:08:44,422
for pleasing us.

222
00:08:45,505 --> 00:08:48,184
In fact, cats often do best when

223
00:08:48,058 --> 00:08:50,121
they're tested at home, in a place

224
00:08:50,015 --> 00:08:52,422
where they feel safe and relaxed.

225
00:08:53,468 --> 00:08:55,754
Chapter nine: how to keep your cat's

226
00:08:55,687 --> 00:08:56,614
brain sharp.

227
00:08:56,993 --> 00:08:59,159
A bored brain can lead to stress,

228
00:08:59,566 --> 00:09:01,587
overeating and bad behavior.

229
00:09:02,646 --> 00:09:05,149
Puzzle feeders make your cat think and

230
00:09:05,074 --> 00:09:07,354
work for its food, just like hunting

231
00:09:07,311 --> 00:09:07,857
does.

232
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:11,041
Rotate toys every few days.

233
00:09:11,431 --> 00:09:13,960
New things are exciting, and old toys

234
00:09:13,912 --> 00:09:16,291
feel new again after a short break.

235
00:09:17,298 --> 00:09:20,329
Clicker training is great brain exercise.

236
00:09:20,707 --> 00:09:23,563
Cats can learn tricks like sit, high-five,

237
00:09:23,852 --> 00:09:26,030
spin, or even ringing a bell.

238
00:09:27,069 --> 00:09:28,713
Window perches are great too.

239
00:09:29,068 --> 00:09:32,318
Watching birds and people outside is basically

240
00:09:32,228 --> 00:09:33,706
television for cats.

241
00:09:34,804 --> 00:09:36,708
Hide treats around the house and let

242
00:09:36,599 --> 00:09:39,029
your cat use its nose to find

243
00:09:38,972 --> 00:09:39,289
them.

244
00:09:39,622 --> 00:09:41,902
It turns snack time into a treasure

245
00:09:41,819 --> 00:09:42,219
hunt.

246
00:09:43,234 --> 00:09:44,886
And keep talking to your cat.

247
00:09:45,284 --> 00:09:47,850
As we saw, cats really do listen,

248
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:49,579
especially to your voice.

249
00:09:50,630 --> 00:09:53,101
Even five minutes of training or play

250
00:09:53,014 --> 00:09:55,003
a day can make a big difference

251
00:09:54,905 --> 00:09:56,775
for an indoor cat's happiness.

252
00:09:57,878 --> 00:09:59,452
So, is your cat smart?

253
00:09:59,849 --> 00:10:01,132
Science says yes.

254
00:10:01,564 --> 00:10:04,934
Just in a very cat way: independent,

255
00:10:05,359 --> 00:10:08,167
observant, and always one step ahead of

256
00:10:08,037 --> 00:10:08,865
the treat bag.

257
00:10:09,881 --> 00:10:13,251
Your cat is reading your face, remembering

258
00:10:13,152 --> 00:10:15,746
your routine, and keeping track of where

259
00:10:15,639 --> 00:10:18,080
you are, all while pretending not to

260
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:18,535
care.

261
00:10:19,565 --> 00:10:21,051
How smart is your cat?

262
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:23,231
Tell me the smartest thing your cat

263
00:10:23,209 --> 00:10:25,449
has ever done in the comments, and

264
00:10:25,359 --> 00:10:27,060
I'll see you in the next video.
